sonal loans, overdrafts, or buy-now-pay-later. In the UK, the Gambling Levy Regulations (in force since April 2025) turned a discretionary industry contribution into a statutory charge that raised almost £120M in its first year, split across treatment, prevention, and research. In Victoria, Australia, slot machines now require a registered card with loss and time limits set before play even starts.\n\nThe Responsible Gambling Council's new report, \"Evolving Expectations\" - the first in its six-part Sector Signals series, published July 27 - lines these three markets up side by side. Different legal tools, same direction: what used to be a VIP program's internal judgment call is now a regulator's hard rule, enforced before the first spin.\n\nFor VIP operations, the read-through is simple. The operators still winning aren't the ones lobbying against these frameworks - they're the ones already running compliant-by-default programs that don't need a regulator to catch up with them.\n\nIf your VIP tier still relies on discretion instead of design, the window to fix that quietly is closing.\n\n#iGaming #VIPRetention #CasinoOperations #PlayerExperience"}}